WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.640
So tell us a little bit about all
the many things that the MBTA does.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.318
&gt;&gt; The MBTA's basic service is,

00:00:06.318 --> 00:00:10.780
it provides 1.3 million trips every day
to people throughout Greater Boston.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.263
It does this through
an extensive bus system,

00:00:14.263 --> 00:00:18.103
four subway lines,
a huge commuter rail system and

00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:22.044
as well as a handful of ferries.
&gt;&gt; How does the age of Boston's subway

00:00:22.044 --> 00:00:23.231
system come into play here?

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:27.117
How does it matter for you?
&gt;&gt; It's really the issue that our

00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:31.227
customers care that the subway and
the bus is reliable and

00:00:31.227 --> 00:00:35.265
the age of the system sort of
strikes at the core of that.

00:00:35.265 --> 00:00:39.246
We have one rail line with cars from 1946.

00:00:39.246 --> 00:00:44.584
We have cars that only 12 years old that
are not performing as well as we hoped and

00:00:44.584 --> 00:00:48.117
it really affects the reliability
of the system, and

00:00:48.117 --> 00:00:53.012
I think what folks want is a train or
a bus that shows up on time on schedule.

00:00:53.012 --> 00:00:55.721
And gets them where they
wanna go In the amount of

00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:58.710
time that they've been
told it's gonna take them.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.380
I think making investments in
the system is the key to doing that.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.740
Or we're bringing in hundreds of new
cars for the red and orange line.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.850
And as a consequence, the newer,

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.790
the new technology is gonna require
us to upgrade the track and signals.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.900
We're gonna get to the point where,

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.970
probably by 2023 where we can have
three minute headways on the Red Line.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:22.470
Right now we have four to six minutes
headways at about 75% reliability.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.940
I think we can get to three minute
headways at 95% reliabiity.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.740
So all the way up and down the Red Line,
you're gonna be plus or

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.000
minus 90 seconds from
a train during rush hour.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.120
I think that's gonna be
a real benefit to customers.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.660
I also think it's gonna
bring marginal customers

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.650
who might be considering
an alternative mode of transportation.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.720
It's gonna bring marginal
customers into the system.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.570
So I think these type of investments
is what's gonna drive new ridership and

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.500
provide better service to
our existing ridership.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.440
&gt;&gt; And how is this paid for?

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.461
Surely the subway passengers can't
possibly be expected to pay for

00:01:51.461 --> 00:01:54.590
the upgrading of their own service.
&gt;&gt; We did raise fares so

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.210
they are in fact paying for some it.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.500
But we have capital funding available from
the State through internally generated

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.150
funds and

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.910
as well as through Federal Funds.
&gt;&gt; What is internally generated

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.490
funds mean?
&gt;&gt; We essentially have

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:08.890
the capacity to borrow.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:12.110
So we borrow to fund certain projects.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:12.705
One of the-
&gt;&gt; So

00:02:12.705 --> 00:02:16.572
you think they'll actually be paid for by
future customers, is that what that means?

00:02:16.572 --> 00:02:18.519
&gt;&gt; They will be paid for, yes,

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:21.730
they will be paid for by future customers.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:25.550
There's also been some
dedicated state funding

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.580
as well that is gonna pay for them.
&gt;&gt; This is an area where the trade off

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.870
between rail and bus becomes quite clear
where you could clearly do some version of

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:36.670
what's going on in these 46 and
56 trains on buses instead.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.780
How should I think more generally
about that trade off in transportation

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:40.620
systems?
&gt;&gt; I mean,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.950
I think there's attractive
features to bus, right?

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.089
You have the flexibility
to redesign your system.

00:02:46.089 --> 00:02:49.370
You have the flexibility
to serve populations that.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.706
And do it in relatively short order and

00:02:51.706 --> 00:02:56.086
we have areas where we are underserving
customers as the Boston area has

00:02:56.086 --> 00:03:00.688
developed there's been a bunch of
transportation nodes that have grown.

00:03:00.688 --> 00:03:04.440
I mean the ones that leap to mind
are particularly north of the city.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.350
We have big populations in East Boston and
Chelsea that are using the bus.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:14.320
We're trying to build capacity in
that area in terms of bus stations

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.970
and large intermodal stations.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:17.898
But also supplement the bus routes.

00:03:17.898 --> 00:03:23.089
On the otherhand, with rail, there is some
virtue in having a fixed rail system,

00:03:23.089 --> 00:03:28.058
particularly in a congested city center
where you can get people rapidly to and

00:03:28.058 --> 00:03:28.974
from a place.

00:03:28.974 --> 00:03:33.206
And I think as we improve service, we
talked about the red line, as we improve

00:03:33.206 --> 00:03:38.050
service on the red line, I think
the benefits of that will become clearer.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.710
The issue with rail now
is construction cost.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:45.680
And available space to
actually build new capacity.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.010
&gt;&gt; Late night service.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.383
So, the T has, in the past,
a familiar battle cry,

00:03:50.383 --> 00:03:55.065
people who are much younger than I am
was that it's horrible for Boston,

00:03:55.065 --> 00:03:57.863
that the T doesn't run later than it does.

00:03:57.863 --> 00:03:59.257
How should I think about this and

00:03:59.257 --> 00:04:03.457
what changes have been made in this area?
&gt;&gt; Yes, the T has gone through several

00:04:03.457 --> 00:04:08.310
iterations of late night service and
I think we're still thinking about

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:12.778
what actually makes sense and
what there's actually demand for.

00:04:12.778 --> 00:04:17.425
One of the issues with the previous late
night service was there was relatively low

00:04:17.425 --> 00:04:17.972
demand.

00:04:17.972 --> 00:04:22.355
I think the one thing that folks don't
think about because they are not, they

00:04:22.355 --> 00:04:27.214
shouldn't necessarily but in a system with
really unbelievable state of good repair

00:04:27.214 --> 00:04:31.690
needs where we have to do so much work on
this system to get it up into condition.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:36.785
Running trains until 1 or
2 in the morning on a service that opens

00:04:36.785 --> 00:04:41.619
up at 5:00 am,
really constrains your window to do work.

00:04:41.619 --> 00:04:44.627
You also have to think about what
populations you want to serve.

00:04:44.627 --> 00:04:49.840
Is this a vehicle to promote night life
in Boston or is is for service workers

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:55.160
who have limited transportation options
who need to get to and from places.

